# The network of "The circuit Google names": the shop's depot, with a
# router of its own and two private circuits to one cloud.
#
#   pc 10.2.0.10 -- site -- depot (AS 65002) ==ic1 169.254.50.0/30== cloud (AS 64900) -- vpc -- app 10.60.0.10
#                                           \==ic2 169.254.70.0/30==/
#
# ic1 and ic2 stand in for two VLAN attachments of a Google Cloud
# Interconnect: each is a cable with a BGP session over it. The cloud's
# router is scenery, configured and saved, and follows Cloud Router's
# documented rules (the prompt gives them); depot is the learner's. The
# cloud announces its network with a MED of 200 over ic1 and 100 over ic2,
# so ic2 is the circuit it names as the primary. ic1's addresses are the
# lower ones, so when everything else about two paths is equal, the tie
# goes to ic1 on both routers (FRR also shares the traffic over two equal
# paths): the lab can't be passed by leaving things as they are, nor by
# taking the first circuit for the primary. Each router's interfaces are
# named after the cable or network at the far end. Addresses come from the
# private ranges and the link-local block for circuits (169.254.0.0/16), as
# the clouds use, and AS numbers from the private range (RFC 6996).
machine pc
machine app
machine depot router
machine cloud router

lan site pc:eth0=10.2.0.10/24 depot:lan=10.2.0.1/24
link depot:ic1=169.254.50.1/30 cloud:ic1=169.254.50.2/30
link depot:ic2=169.254.70.1/30 cloud:ic2=169.254.70.2/30
lan vpc cloud:vpc=10.60.0.1/16 app:eth0=10.60.0.10/16

route pc default via 10.2.0.1
route app default via 10.60.0.1

run depot frr
run cloud frr
run pc web-svc
run app web-svc
